canonicalUrl https://patrickcollison.com/svhistory

post-header.scroll

In response to a question from Tanner Greer, a first cut at what a Silicon Valley canon might look like.
 linkify false
 link https://x.com/patrickc/status/1825618450837885036 first cut

Alan Kay says that computing is pop culture -- "pop culture holds a disdain for history". The below are my favorite books and articles about the history of computing and Silicon Valley:
 linkify false
 link http://www.drdobbs.com/architecture-and-design/interview-with-alan-kay/240003442 says

- The Dream Machine -- probably the best overall book about the early days of computing and the internet.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/The-Dream-Machine-Licklider-Revolution/dp/014200135X The Dream Machine

- Heat Death: Venture Capital in the 1980s -- a brief history of the venture capital industry since 1973.
 linkify false
 link http://reactionwheel.net/2015/01/80s-vc.html Heat Death: Venture Capital in the 1980s

- Genentech: The Beginnings of Biotech -- the definitive history of Genentech.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/Genentech-The-Beginnings-Biotech-Synthesis/dp/022604551X Genentech: The Beginnings of Biotech

- The Whole Earth Catalog -- the library at PARC was first composed simply of the books that the Whole Earth Catalog recommended. Steve Jobs cites it as inspiration for Apple. It's still possible to find original copies.
 linkify false
 link http://www.wholeearth.com/index.php The Whole Earth Catalog

- Dealers of Lightning -- the best history of PARC.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/Dealers-Lightning-Xerox-PARC-Computer/dp/0887309895 Dealers of Lightning

- The Idea Factory -- sets the context for the hardware inventions that directly led to Silicon Valley.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/The-Idea-Factory-American-Innovation/dp/0143122797 The Idea Factory

- Computer Lib -- one of the greatest books about the possibilities of computers ever written.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/Computer-Lib-Dream-Machines/dp/0893470031 Computer Lib

- Man-Computer Symbiosis -- established the vision of computing that inspired Engelbart, Kay, etc., to pursue what they did.
 linkify false
 link http://memex.org/licklider.pdf Man-Computer Symbiosis

- The Chip -- the invention of the microchip.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/The-Chip-Americans-Microchip-Revolution/dp/0375758283 The Chip

- Something Ventured -- a lighthearted history of the venture capital industry.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/Something-Ventured-Tom-Perkins/dp/B0079ZWUW0/ Something Ventured

- The Little Kingdom -- the early days of Apple Computer and the Macintosh.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/Little-Kingdom-Private-Story-Computer/dp/0688039731/ The Little Kingdom

- From Counterculture to Cyberculture -- Stewart Brand and his surrounding milieu.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/0226817423/ From Counterculture to Cyberculture

- Season of the Witch -- a cultural history of San Francisco from the late 60s to the early 80s.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/Season-Witch-Enchantment-Terror-Deliverance/dp/1439108242/ Season of the Witch

- Making Silicon Valley -- there aren't many books that say a lot about the Valley pre-Shockley; this one goes back to the 30s.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/Making-Silicon-Valley-Innovation-Technology/dp/0262622114 Making Silicon Valley

- The Tinkerings of Robert Noyce -- the origins and cultural context of Fairchild and Intel.
 linkify false
 link https://web.stanford.edu/class/e145/2007_fall/materials/noyce.html The Tinkerings of Robert Noyce

- Revolution in The Valley -- a hundred stories from the early days of the Mac, collected by one of its creators.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/Revolution-The-Valley-Insanely-Great/dp/1449316247 Revolution in The Valley

- Swimming Across -- Andy Grove's memoir.
 linkify false
 link http://www.amazon.com/Swimming-Across-Andrew-S-Grove/dp/0446529923 Swimming Across

There's a closely-related genre of great books (like Masters of Doom and Show-Stopper) that cover important projects in the industry's history. Here, though, I've omitted those that don't have a direct connection to Silicon Valley.
 linkify false

